              <tr><td>Fiction</td><td>240</td><td>43.6%</td><td>$10.85</td><td>4.65</td><td>15,684</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Non-Fiction</td><td>310</td><td>56.4%</td><td>$14.84</td><td>4.60</td><td>9,065</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div>
          <canvas id="genreBar" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Non-Fiction contributes a larger share of bestseller entries, but at materially higher average prices.</li>
        <li>Fiction combines lower price points with slightly stronger ratings and significantly higher review depth.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section card" id="price-rating-correlation">
      <h2>Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
      <p><strong>Pearson correlation coefficient (Price, User Rating): -0.13</strong>. This indicates a weak negative relationship: higher price does not systematically produce higher user ratings within this bestseller set.</p>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div><canvas id="scatter" height="280"></canvas></div>
        <div>
          <canvas id="segmentChart" height="280"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>The rating spread remains relatively tight across most price bands, reinforcing that content fit and audience resonance matter more than price alone.</li>
        <li>$16–20 titles show the lowest average ratings (4.50), while both low-price ($0–5) and select premium tiers ($21–30) sustain strong ratings.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section card" id="genre-comparison">
      <h2>Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Fiction</th><th>Non-Fiction</th><th>Management Read</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Average Price</td><td>$10.85</td><td>$14.84</td><td>Non-Fiction holds pricing premium (+$3.99).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average User Rating</td><td>4.65</td><td>4.60</td><td>Fiction leads modestly in satisfaction (+0.05).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average Reviews</td><td>15,684</td><td>9,065</td><td>Fiction exhibits deeper customer engagement.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Volume Share</td><td>43.6%</td><td>56.4%</td><td>Non-Fiction dominates list presence.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:8px;">Value signal: Fiction appears to deliver stronger perceived value (higher ratings at lower price), while Non-Fiction appears to monetize authority/utility positioning.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section card" id="temporal-trends">
      <h2>Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
      <p><strong>Critical inflection points:</strong> 2015 shows the sharpest pricing reset (average price dropped to $10.42 from $14.64 in 2014), and 2019 marks the strongest rating step-up (average rating rose to 4.74, highest in period).</p>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div><canvas id="trendLine" height="300"></canvas></div>
        <div><canvas id="reviewTrend" height="300"></canvas></div>
      </div>
      <table style="margin-top:12px;">
        <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Avg Reviews</th></tr></thead>
